{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-143380",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{x}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
